<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "Seven supervised learning models (linear regression, ridge regression, lasso regression, elastic net, decision tree, support vector regression, and random forest) were trained to predict wind speed at 102.2 m using mesoscale data, meteorological-mast observations, or both data sources. Their performance was assessed using five-fold cross-validation and compared with the power-law method." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "Machine-learning models trained with mesoscale data, observed data, or both were reported to outperform the power-law method for MAE, MSE, RMSE, and R2. Random Forest achieved the best reported result for scenario A using mesoscale data only. For scenario B, Random Forest had the highest tabulated R2, but linear regression was recommended because its performance was similar while its computational cost was substantially lower. For scenario C, Ridge regression was recommended because Random Forest and SVR did not provide considerable improvement relative to their computational cost." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__objective_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__objective_002> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#employsAnalysis> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__analysis_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#investigatesResearchProblem> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__problem_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapIdentified> "Few studies have focused on vertical wind-speed extrapolation in the temporal domain, and further comparison of representative supervised-learning algorithms and the use of modeled mesoscale atmospheric data were needed." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "MAE" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "MSE" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "RMSE" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R2> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapAddressed> "The study compared seven supervised-learning algorithms for the same vertical wind-speed extrapolation task and incorporated modeled data from the New European Wind Atlas into the training data." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378020__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyFutureWorks> "Determine the confidence level and uncertainty of wind-resource assessments using machine learning instead of the power law, assess whether measurement campaigns can be partially avoided when modeled data alone are used, investigate optimized coarse-resolution mesoscale models, and improve Random Forest using high-performance computing or ensemble approaches." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "The study trained and compared LASSO, Random Forest, and XGBoost transfer methods for constructing 3-hourly 100 m wind speed from ERA5 near-surface wind and other meteorological, geographical, terrain, and time features." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "The methods were evaluated against ERA5 using a 2004–2005 test set, and the best-performing Random Forest method was applied to 19 Euro-CORDEX regional climate projections under RCP8.5 and to two mast stations." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "The machine-learning methods produced more accurate 100 m wind-speed estimates than the traditional power-law method, with Random Forest having the highest overall simulation accuracy among the three tested machine-learning algorithms." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "Random Forest and XGBoost reproduced the diurnal and seasonal changes of 100 m wind speed more accurately than the traditional method." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "The regional mean Random Forest RMSE was 0.525, corresponding to a reported 64.1% reduction relative to the traditional method." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "Under RCP8.5, the study found significant negative trends in European hub-height wind speed and wind energy during 1979–2019 and projected further reductions in wind-power production." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "At the SAC mast station, the transfer-model predictions systematically underestimated 100 m wind speed by about 40%, indicating limitations when local surface conditions differ from the ERA5 grid representation." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__objective_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__objective_002> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__objective_003> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#conductedAt> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__location_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#conductedAt> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__location_002> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#conductedAt> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__location_003> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#employsAnalysis> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__analysis_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#employsAnalysis> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__analysis_002> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#investigatesResearchProblem> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__problem_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapIdentified> "Hourly hub-height wind-speed output is rare in global and regional climate models, which often provide only 10 m wind speed, while constant-exponent extrapolation does not represent the nonlinear, spatially and temporally varying relationship between surface and hub-height winds." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "MAE" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "RMSE" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "MSE" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "KolmogorovSmirnovStatistic" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapAddressed> "The study develops a data-driven transfer method to construct gridded hub-height wind speed from commonly available climate-model variables over a large region." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyFutureWorks> "Future developments could apply more elaborate bias-correction methods to climate projections." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyFutureWorks> "Performance at specific sites could be improved using smaller-scale models and additional local information." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyFutureWorks> "The method could be combined with short periods of 100 m wind measurements and long periods of surface-wind measurements to produce long-term hub-height wind-speed series for site assessment." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyLimits> "ERA5 has known near-surface wind mismatches and a general underestimation bias." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyLimits> "The transfer-model accuracy decreases near coastlines, suggesting that distance from the coastline could be included as an additional feature." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyLimits> "Only two long-term mast stations were used as examples because long mast records are rare." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyLimits> "The ERA5-trained transfer model may be inaccurate at sites where adjacent vegetation, buildings, altitude, or local land use cause surface-wind observations to differ from ERA5." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378024__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyLimits> "The climate-model application used a simple bias-correction technique, although more elaborate methods are available." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "The study developed an LSTM-based vertical wind-speed extrapolation procedure trained first with the Levenberg-Marquardt method and enhanced using particle swarm optimization. Measured 10-minute-average LiDAR wind speeds from 10 to 40 m were used iteratively to estimate wind speed from 50 to 120 m. PSO-LSTM was compared with standard LSTM, FFNN, Log Law, and several alternative optimization algorithms." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "PSO-LSTM outperformed standard LSTM, FFNN, and Log Law in most performance measures at all evaluated heights. Its R2 values were 96.35%, 88.12%, and 78.26% at 60, 90, and 120 m, respectively. Errors generally increased and performance deteriorated with increasing extrapolation height. PSO-LSTM's superiority did not apply uniformly to MBE: FFNN had the lowest MBE at 60 and 80 m, while PSO-LSTM performed better at the remaining heights." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__objective_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__objective_002> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#conductedAt> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__location_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#investigatesResearchProblem> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__problem_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapIdentified> "The utilization of machine-learning methods to predict wind speed at hub height from measured wind speed at lower heights is limited, and most prior vertical wind-speed extrapolation studies used classical methods." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "RMSE" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "MBE" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "MAPE" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R2> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "PearsonCorrelation" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "coefficient of variance (COV)" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapAddressed> "The study proposed and evaluated a particle-swarm-optimization-enhanced LSTM method for vertical wind-speed extrapolation and compared it with standard LSTM, FFNN, Log Law, and alternative optimization methods." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyFutureWorks> "Further explore the applicability of PSO-LSTM at other sites if measured data is available." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyLimits> "Only measured wind speeds between 10 and 40 m were used to estimate wind speeds at all higher heights, and deviations from measurements increased with height." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R1378028__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyLimits> "Applicability at other sites was not established in this study." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689577__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689577__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689577__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "Hourly wind-speed data from 27 INMET automatic monitoring stations in Mato Grosso do Sul were cleaned, grouped using Ward cluster analysis, and fitted with a two-parameter Weibull distribution. Six numerical parameter-estimation methods were compared using RMSE, R2, and the Kolmogorov-Smirnov statistic." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689577__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "Maximum likelihood and modified maximum likelihood were the most accurate methods for most analyzed stations, although the best of these two depended on month and station group. Modified maximum likelihood performed best for most months in Group 3, while maximum likelihood performed best at Três Lagoas. At low-wind-speed stations, method accuracy decreased" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689577__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "the graphical method was best at Coxim from September to February. The Weibull distribution was not recommended for Coxim and Miranda, which were characterized by low monthly average wind speeds." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689577__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689577__objective_001> .
