<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689577__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R2> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689577__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "KolmogorovSmirnovStatistic" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689577__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapAddressed> "The study compared six Weibull parameter-estimation methods using similar wind-speed series separated into calibration and accuracy-analysis series." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689577__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyLimits> "The number of monitoring points was below the recommended number for an area of this extent." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689577__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyLimits> "The Weibull distribution showed reduced suitability at stations characterized by low monthly average wind speeds." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689596__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689596__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689596__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "Fourteen methods for estimating the shape and scale parameters of a two-parameter Weibull distribution were compared using hourly wind data from 2005–2014 measured or represented at 10 m for three slightly, moderately, and very windy sites. Performance was assessed with RMSE, R2, and relative error" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689596__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "skewness, kurtosis, wind power density, mean wind speed, standard deviation, and height extrapolation were also analysed." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689596__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "Among the methods assessed at the three selected sites, the Energy Pattern Factor Method had the best RMSE and R2 performance and was the most suitable method for determining Weibull parameters." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689596__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "The Wind Variability Method and Moroccan Method were not appropriate for estimating Weibull parameters at the three sites." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689596__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "The Moment Method produced the lowest relative error when estimating standard deviation at the study sites." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689596__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "The calculated distributions had positive skewness and were skewed to the right, while their kurtosis coefficients were negative." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689596__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "For the extrapolation methodology used in this study, the Weibull shape parameter decreased with height above ground level while the scale parameter increased with height." .
