<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689600__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689600__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689600__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "Wind-speed data for 1992–2010 from twelve Nigerian meteorological stations at 10 m above ground level were analyzed with Weibull, mixture Weibull, Gamma, lognormal and normal distributions. Parameters were estimated using maximum likelihood, and model performance was compared using Kolmogorov–Smirnov, RMSE and chi-square goodness-of-fit tests. Although the paper consistently describes five distributions, the conclusion refers to a comparison of four distribution models." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689600__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "Across the twelve study stations, mixture Weibull was ranked first by the Kolmogorov–Smirnov test and was also ranked first by chi-square at all stations except Minna and Ikeja. RMSE ranked lognormal first at all stations except Minna. In the overall ranking, mixture Weibull ranked first under Kolmogorov–Smirnov and chi-square, whereas lognormal ranked first under RMSE. The authors selected mixture Weibull as the best method overall and concluded that the conventional Weibull and Gamma methods performed poorly for the study locations." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689600__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R689600__objective_001> .
