<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707542__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyLimits> "The MKD model is not applicable for wind-speed probability-distribution estimation in situations where wind-speed data are not available" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707542__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyLimits> "this shortcoming is inherited from the kernel density model." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707550__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707550__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707550__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "Hourly measured wind-speed data from ten stations in Ontario, Canada, were used to fit and compare the two-parameter Birnbaum-Saunders distribution with nine one-component probability distributions. Distribution parameters were computed using the maximum likelihood method, and goodness of fit was assessed using R2, RMSE, AIC, and BIC. Wind-power-density distributions were subsequently estimated from the fitted wind-speed distributions." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707550__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "The BS distribution showed very favorable performance for estimating wind-speed and wind-power-density distributions at the examined stations. It gave the best fit at six stations. The paper contains conflicting statements regarding its rank at the remaining four stations: the abstract reports third or fourth, whereas the results and conclusions report third or fifth. Weibull was reported as the second-best function for wind-power-density distribution but was not able to give an adequate description of wind-speed-data frequencies at all sites." .
