<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707550__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapIdentified> "To the best of the authors' knowledge, no investigation had applied the Birnbaum-Saunders function to estimate wind-speed and wind-power distributions." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707550__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R2> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707550__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "RMSE" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707550__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "AIC" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707550__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "BIC" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707550__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapAddressed> "The study introduced and evaluated the two-parameter Birnbaum-Saunders distribution for wind-speed and wind-power-density distributions." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707550__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyFutureWorks> "Future wind-resource-assessment work could consider the applicability of additional distribution functions to certain sites and should use a number of case studies with different wind-speed and wind-power characteristics." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "The study fitted fourteen probability distributions to 10-min mean wind-speed samples from 178 North American ocean buoys. Distributional performance was compared using probability-plot R2, estimates of average wind-turbine power output, and estimates of extreme wind-speed exceedances." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "The bimodal Weibull, Kappa, and Wakeby distributions provided significantly better R2 fits than the other distributions considered, with the bimodal Weibull giving the highest R2 values. Kappa gave the lowest mean-square error and bias for average turbine-power estimates, whereas the 2-parameter Lognormal gave the best extreme-wind-speed estimates but still had significant errors. Model selection therefore depends on the wind-energy application." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__objective_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__objective_002> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#conductedAt> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__location_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#employsAnalysis> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__analysis_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#employsAnalysis> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__analysis_002> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#employsAnalysis> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__analysis_003> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#investigatesResearchProblem> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__problem_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#investigatesResearchProblem> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__problem_002> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapIdentified> "Previous mixture-distribution studies used limited samples from confined geographic regions, and conventional goodness-of-fit measures such as R2 do not necessarily indicate how well a model predicts wind-energy parameters." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R2> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapAddressed> "The study used a larger geographically diverse offshore dataset and compared distribution performance using both goodness-of-fit and engineering-parameter estimation metrics." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyFutureWorks> "Further analyses are needed to validate models based on application-specific engineering parameters, including average power output, extreme wind speed, and potentially fatigue loads." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyLimits> "All samples were from ocean environments, which may limit the utility of some findings for onshore data. Confidence intervals for estimated exceedance counts were challenging to construct because the observations could not be considered independent." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#assessesEnergySystem> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707558__energysystem_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707568__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707568__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707568__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "The study analyzed 10-min wind data from Shurje, Iran, evaluated 46 continuous probability distribution functions using Kolmogorov-Smirnov and R2 measures, extrapolated wind speed to 80 m using power-law and logarithmic-law methods, analyzed turbulence intensity and surface roughness, and estimated 50-year extreme wind speed using periodic maxima, a Gumbel distribution, and the Harris algorithm." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707568__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "Among the evaluated distribution functions, Wakeby had the greatest compatibility with the measured wind data. Estimated 80 m wind speed using the power law ranged from 5.58 to 12.24 m/s, with an annual mean of 7.72 m/s. The estimated 50-year extreme wind speed at 80 m was 44.262 m/s" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707568__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "the authors concluded that this value was lower than typical Ve50 limits and that the site had limited risk from extreme weather conditions." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707568__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707568__objective_001> .
