<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707568__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyFutureWorks> "Future work was proposed to investigate extreme wind speed using the method of independent storms and the EWTS II algorithm" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707568__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyFutureWorks> "optimize Wakeby coefficients using nature-inspired algorithms" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707568__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyFutureWorks> "analyze uncertainty in wind-speed extrapolation and relations between shear parameters and atmospheric stability" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707568__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyFutureWorks> "explore neural networks for stable-boundary-layer wind-speed fitting" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707568__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyFutureWorks> "assess further meteorological datasets" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707568__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyFutureWorks> "and apply a measure-correlate-predict method." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707578__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707578__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707578__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "The study fitted 11 one-component parametric probability density functions, two two-component mixture distributions, and a kernel-density non-parametric distribution to hourly average wind-speed series from nine meteorological stations in the UAE. Suitability was evaluated using log-likelihood, R2 based on P-P and Q-Q plots, Chi-square, and Kolmogorov-Smirnov statistics." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707578__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "Overall, mixture distributions were generally the best probability density functions according to every statistic, with MWW more suitable than MGG most of the time. KAP and GG were the best one-component distributions overall. W2 was the best two-parameter distribution and performed better than some three-parameter distributions such as GEV and LN3. One-component probability density functions were not suitable for wind-speed distributions presenting severe bimodal regimes." .
