<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707692__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707692__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707692__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "The study compared ten two-component homogeneous and heterogeneous mixture distributions, constructed from Gamma, Weibull, Extreme value type-one, and Normal distributions, with Weibull and four-parameter Kappa distributions for hourly mean wind speed data at seven UAE stations. Mixture parameters were estimated using Maximum Likelihood, Expectation Maximization, and Least Squares methods" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707692__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "Genetic Algorithm optimization was used for the Maximum Likelihood and Least Squares objective functions. Goodness of fit was assessed using BIC, adjusted R2, and chi-squared statistics." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707692__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "Mixture distributions provided the best fit to wind speed data at all seven UAE stations according to the employed goodness-of-fit measures. The Weibull-Extreme value type-one mixture distribution was concluded to be the most appropriate distribution for wind speed data in the UAE. For stations with skewed unimodality, the improvement from heterogeneous mixture distributions was less important than for stations with skewed bimodality and kurtotic unimodality, and non-mixture distributions could compete with mixture distributions." .
