<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707692__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapAddressed> "The study investigated the applicability of heterogeneous mixture distributions and identified an appropriate distribution for modeling wind speed data in the UAE." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707701__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707701__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707701__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "The study fitted and evaluated Johnson-Wehrly, Farlie-Gumbel-Morgenstern, and Plackett bivariate distributions for joint wind-speed and wind-direction data from six Mediterranean locations. Wind-speed marginal distributions included conventional and mixture distributions, while wind direction was represented using finite mixtures of von Mises distributions. Bivariate fits were evaluated using seven bin-specific measures." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707701__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "Across the examined locations and criteria, the Johnson-Wehrly family generally provided the best bivariate fits relative to the Farlie-Gumbel-Morgenstern and Plackett families. The univariate best-fit wind-speed distribution did not necessarily provide the best bivariate wind-speed--wind-direction fit. The selected Johnson-Wehrly models were applied to estimate wind-power-density distributions at Andros, Pylos, and Mar de Alboran." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707701__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707701__objective_001> .
