<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707701__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapIdentified> "A more in-depth assessment and evaluation of bivariate analytic distribution families was needed to investigate their efficiency and capability for modelling in-situ wind-speed and wind-direction data from diverse wind regimes." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707701__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "RMSE" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707701__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "MAE" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707701__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "ChiSquareStatistic" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707701__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R2> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707701__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "RRMSE" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707701__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "Index of agreement" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707701__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "Normalized deviation" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707701__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapAddressed> "The study compared three bivariate parametric distribution families at six Mediterranean locations using seven bin-specific goodness-of-fit measures." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707701__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyFutureWorks> "The authors suggest investigating suitable bivariate copulas and kernel density functions, and considering alternative circular models such as nonnegative trigonometric sums for modelling the dependence structure in the Johnson-Wehrly model." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707701__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyLimits> "The authors note that multivariate goodness-of-fit tools are limited and that the evaluation measures used are bin-specific. Although several wind-speed and wind-direction bin widths were tested, further research on joint distributions is needed to improve goodness of fit." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707709__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707709__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707709__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "The study fitted 23 analytic probability distributions to long-term offshore wind-speed time series from 19 Mediterranean buoys. Fits were assessed using Kolmogorov–Smirnov, Anderson–Darling and chi-square goodness-of-fit tests, followed by coefficient of determination R2. Selected fitted distributions were additionally evaluated by relative estimation errors for mean wind power density and average wind turbine power output." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707709__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "Johnson SB, Kappa and Wakeby distributions accurately described the empirical offshore wind-speed distributions at the examined Eastern and Western Mediterranean locations and had better adaptability than the Weibull distribution. Johnson SB was the only distribution reported to suit very well in all examined cases with consistently fair goodness-of-fit results. However, the distribution providing the best wind-speed fit was not necessarily the best solution for wind-energy characteristics such as mean wind power density or average wind turbine power output." .
