<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707902__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "MBE" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R707902__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyLimits> "The methodology requires near-surface wind-speed time series measured in meteorological networks, a digital terrain model, a land-cover dataset, and wind-speed data not influenced by local topography or land use for portability." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708241__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708241__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708241__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "The study fitted two- and three-parameter Weibull distributions (WD2 and WD3), a skewed generalized error distribution (SGED), and a skewed t distribution (STD) to hourly wind-speed data from four Turkish stations. Model performance was compared for monthly, seasonal, and yearly wind-speed data using R2, RMSE, Kolmogorov-Smirnov statistic, negative log likelihood, and AIC." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708241__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "For the monthly, seasonal, and yearly analyses considered, SGED and STD were generally more suitable than WD2 and WD3 according to the considered model-selection criteria. Particularly, SGED provided the best fit to a variety of wind-speed data. For Station 4 yearly data, STD had the highest fit according to R2, RMSE, and KS, whereas WD3 was best according to negative log likelihood and AIC." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708241__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708241__objective_001> .
