<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708260__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#assessesEnergySystem> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708260__energysystem_004> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "Daily wind-speed data from 2010–2014 at three Bohaiwan sites in China were characterized and fitted with Weibull, Rayleigh, Lognormal, Gamma, and Loglogistic distributions. The five distributions were compared using RMSE. Seven Weibull-parameter estimation methods, comprising GWO, PSO, CS, EMJ, EML, EPFM, and MLM, were evaluated using IA, RRMSE, and R2. Wind power density, effective wind power density, and wind-turbine availability factor were then calculated using Weibull parameters obtained by GWO." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "Among the five tested distributions, the Weibull distribution had the minimum RMSE for wind-speed data from all three Bohaiwan sites, whereas the Lognormal distribution had the maximum RMSE in every reported period. GWO outperformed the other parameter-estimation methods for almost all considered wind-speed data according to R2, RRMSE, and IA" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "however, for site A during Oct.–Dec., PSO produced the best outcomes, and for site C during Jul.–Sep., CS was the best method. The study concludes that wind-energy resources at the three sites are abundant and that wind turbines are highly likely to operate under the assumed cut-in and cut-out speeds." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__objective_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__objective_002> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#conductedAt> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__location_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#conductedAt> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__location_002> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#conductedAt> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__location_003> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#employsAnalysis> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__analysis_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#employsAnalysis> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708270__analysis_002> .
