<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R708940__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapAddressed> "The study evaluated EGLD as an alternative flexible wind-speed distribution with two shape parameters, one scale parameter and an explicit cumulative distribution function." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R709006__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R709006__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R709006__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "The study fitted eight probability distributions to wind-speed data from four Algerian stations using the Method of Moments, Maximum Likelihood Method, Power Density Method, and L-Moments Method. Goodness of fit was assessed using R2, RMSE, and power density error." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R709006__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "The best PDF/PEM combinations were Gamma/L-MM for Bouzaréah and El-Hamdania, GEV/MLM for Bousmaïl, and GEV/L-MM for Adrar. Across the employed distributions and sites, L-MM was the most accurate parameter-estimation method, followed by MLM, PDM, and MM. GEV/L-MM was the most accurate of the thirty PDF/PEM combinations, whereas LN/PDM gave the worst results. In terms of power density error, PDM with Gamma, Inverse Gaussian, and Log Normal gave different best results from R2 and RMSE." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R709006__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R709006__objective_001> .
