<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R716871__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapAddressed> "While statistical data are presented in many studies in the literature, this study supported statistical data by comparison with real data and compared results from different Weibull-distribution methods." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R716871__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyFutureWorks> "Future studies related to wind energy can investigate wind farm layout optimization, economic feasibility, and environmental impacts to develop wind farm projects." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R717690__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R717690__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R717690__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "The study applied a two-component mixture Weibull distribution to hourly mean wind-speed data from four weather stations on Gran Canaria and compared parameter estimation by the method of moments, maximum likelihood, and least squares. Fits to wind-speed and wind-power-density cumulative distributions were evaluated using SSE, R2, MSE, and RMSE." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R717690__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "The two-component mixture Weibull distribution provided a good fit at all weather stations under study." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R717690__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "For wind-speed distributions, the least-squares method provided the highest degree of fit at all analysed stations, although the difference with the other estimation techniques was not significant." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R717690__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "For wind-power-density distributions, least squares had a slightly higher fit at the unimodal stations 1 and 2" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R717690__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "maximum likelihood and the method of moments had the highest fit at the bimodal station 4 and the bitangential station 3, respectively." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R717690__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "There was no significant difference between the methods employed in describing wind-power probability-density distributions." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R717690__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "If sample data are independent, maximum-likelihood estimators should be used because of their large sampling efficiency, but they require elaborate calculation techniques." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R717690__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "The least-squares method provides a robust and computationally efficient alternative, whereas the method of moments does not always supply a feasible result and lacks the desirable optimality properties of maximum-likelihood and least-squares estimators." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R717690__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R717690__objective_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R717690__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R717690__objective_002> .
