<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R720773__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#reportsCharacterization> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R720773__characterization_003> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R720773__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapIdentified> "Previous assessments of theoretical wind potential at Nigerian locations gave little or no attention to frequency-distribution patterns of wind speed over the studied periods." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R720773__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "PearsonCorrelation" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R720773__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#usesEvaluationMetric> "RMSE" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R720773__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapAddressed> "The study examined frequency distributions of daily average wind speed for Enugu, Onitsha, and Owerri over a longer period of up to 25 years and modeled the observed data using the Weibull distribution." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R720773__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyFutureWorks> "Observations over smaller time-scales, such as hourly averages, may help to improve the results." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R720773__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyLimits> "Daily averages may produce coarse approximations that fail to account for short time-scale variations of wind characteristics." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "Daily average wind-speed data measured at 2 m above ground level were obtained from the Federal University of Kashere weather station for October 2014 to February 2017. The data were checked for homogeneity, outliers, and missing records" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "spurious and missing data, wind gusts, and cyclonic winds exceeding 20 m/s were eliminated. Wind speed was analysed using the Weibull distribution through seasonal and annual analyses, and a vertical wind profile was used to estimate mean wind speed at various heights." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "Monthly average wind speeds computed for 60 m above ground level ranged from 2.15 m/s to 6.42 m/s" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "the maximum occurred in June and the minimum occurred in September." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "The dry season had the highest Weibull shape-parameter value, K = 2.08, which the authors interpreted as signifying more stable winds during that season." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "The modelled average wind speed at 60 m was 4.30 m/s, which the authors stated was beyond a minimum standard of 3.5 m/s." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "The authors concluded that part of the energy requirements of the study area can potentially be obtained from wind throughout the year." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__objective_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__objective_002> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__objective_003> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#conductedAt> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__location_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#employsAnalysis> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__analysis_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#assessesEnergySystem> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R722053__energysystem_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723171__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723171__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723171__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "The study analyzed average daily wind-speed data for Kano, Maiduguri, Jos, Abuja, and Akure using a two-parameter Weibull distribution. It compared maximum likelihood, method of moment, power density, empirical, and logarithmic moment methods for estimating Weibull parameters using RMSE and R2. It also evaluated five 25 kW pitch-controlled wind turbines, including capacity factor and cost of energy, at hub heights from 10 to 50 m." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723171__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "The method of moment was suitable for Kano and Jos, while the empirical method was suitable for Maiduguri and Abuja" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723171__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "both methods were suitable for Akure." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723171__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "The power density method was the worst numerical approach for assessing Weibull parameters across the five locations." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723171__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "Jos and Kano were concluded to be viable for grid integration of wind energy, with Jos identified as the most viable location for wind-energy generation applications." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723171__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "WT3 had the highest capacity factor and the least cost of energy in all selected locations, whereas WT5 had the highest cost of energy." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723171__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "The cost of energy decreased with increasing turbine hub height under the sensitivity-analysis conditions." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723171__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "The wind energy conversion systems have the potential to avoid significant carbon footprints when implemented as alternatives to fossil-fuel systems." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723171__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723171__objective_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723171__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723171__objective_002> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723171__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723171__objective_003> .
