<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723300__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#assessesEnergySystem> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723300__energysystem_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723318__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723318__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723318__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "Four probability distributions were fitted to wind-speed data collected at Yola Airport, Nigeria: Weibull, exponentiated Weibull, generalized power Weibull, and exponentiated epsilon. Parameters were estimated in R using the fitdistrplus package and its default maximum likelihood method. Wind speeds were extrapolated from the 10 m anemometer height to selected heights, and wind-power estimates were calculated using assumed conversion conditions." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723318__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "All four distributions provided good fits to the Yola wind-speed dataset. The exponentiated Weibull, generalized power Weibull, and exponentiated epsilon distributions had relative likelihood gains per data point of 5.25%, 5.38%, and 5.50%, respectively, over the Weibull distribution" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723318__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "the exponentiated epsilon distribution had the highest gain. The authors report that an exponentiated-epsilon estimated average wind speed of 3.68 m/s at 120 m above ground level could generate 6.11 W/m2, and that a proposed 20-turbine wind farm configuration could generate 1.57 MW." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723318__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723318__objective_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723318__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723318__objective_002> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723318__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#conductedAt> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723318__location_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723318__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#investigatesResearchProblem> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723318__problem_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723318__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapIdentified> "The Weibull distribution may fail to properly describe wind-speed data of certain locations and may not be the best model compared with other probability distributions." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723318__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyResearchGapAddressed> "The study compared the Weibull distribution with exponentiated Weibull, generalized power Weibull, and exponentiated epsilon distributions for modeling wind-speed data in Yola, Nigeria." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723373__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723373__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723373__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "Daily wind-speed data from four Nigerian locations, measured at 10 m over 11 years, were characterized using a two-parameter Weibull distribution. Wind speeds were extrapolated to hub heights from 10 m to 90 m using a power-law expression. Wind power density, turbine capacity factor, and unit energy cost were estimated to assess wind-turbine selection and techno-economic viability." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723373__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "Jos, Kano and Ikeja were reported as economically viable for grid integration, whereas P/H was reported as viable for standalone application at 10 m. GE/900s was identified as the most suitable turbine for all sites considered because of its high capacity-factor values. Increasing hub height reduced the electricity cost for Ikeja, P/H and Kano, except for Jos, for which 50 m was reported as the optimum hub height." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723373__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723373__objective_001> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723373__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723373__objective_002> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723373__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#conductedAt> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R723373__location_001> .
