<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R724404__study_001> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#Study> .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R724404__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyDomain> "wind" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R724404__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyMethodsSummary> "The study introduced the two-parameter Topp-Leone Lindley distribution, assessed maximum likelihood, least squares and maximum product spacings estimators using Monte Carlo simulation, and compared TLL with six parametric distributions for long-term measured wind-speed data from ten stations in Inner Mongolia, China." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R724404__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "The TLL distribution provided optimal wind-speed fittings at five selected stations and ranked second or third at the remaining stations. The weighted Lindley distribution also effectively fitted wind-speed data at the selected stations. Although the Weibull distribution did not provide good wind-speed fits at all selected stations, it provided stronger estimation of wind power density" .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R724404__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#studyKeyFindings> "TLL also presented accurate estimates of wind power density." .
<https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R724404__study_001> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/ontology#hasObjective> <https://w3id.org/thirdwave/data/R724404__objective_001> .
